\description{
Utility generic for modifying \code{tileApply()} calls for streamlining extension
with new datatypes and forcing datatype-specific handling to be added in the
expected order.

\strong{Why}
\itemize{
\item \code{tileApply} is a framework function for parallelized computing across tiled
data. It allows dispatch on up to 3 signatures (\code{x}, \code{y}, and \code{tile}),
allowing flexibility, but at the cost of normally having to deal with
combinatorial explosion of methods when extending with a new \code{x} and \code{y}
datatype. Especially the case when considering compatibility with existing
types and specific handling for different \code{tile} types.
\item Setting specific methods using the following example dispatch
chain is ambiguous, with it being unclear whether 1 or 2 is first and whether
both will be reached before 3.
\enumerate{
\item \verb{(ANY, myclass, mytiles)}
\item \verb{(myclass, ANY, mytiles)}
\item \verb{(ANY, ANY, mytiles)}
}
}

\code{redispatch_tileapply()} solves the above problems by modifying \code{tileApply()}
calls based on the classes of \code{x} and \code{y}, appropriate to the provided
\verb{tile*} type.

This is done by passing \code{x} or \code{y} as input \code{sig} alongside the \code{tiles} and
dispatching on the combination, and properly routing changes when they are
\code{x} or \code{y} param specific based on the \code{param_xy}.

After a \code{sig} is processed, it is coerced to the \link[=token-class]{token} class signifying
that it is ready for processing and the call is passed back to \code{tileApply()}
which can either proceed with eval or pass back to \code{redispatch_tileapply} if
\code{y} is also provided.

Only when \code{x} (for single input tileApply()) or \code{x} and \code{y} are all of class
\code{token}, does the \code{tileApply()} call fully evaluate.

\strong{The default \code{ANY}, \code{ANY} method performs no modifications to the input data
or the call.}
}
\section{Logic flow between \code{tileApply()} and \code{redispatch_tileapply()}}{


\strong{\code{x} only case:}
\enumerate{
\item \code{tileApply(ANY, missing, ANY, ...)}
\item \code{redispatch_tileapply(mydata, mytiles, param_xy = "x", ...)}
\item \code{redispatch_tileapply(ANY, ANY, param_xy = "x", ...)}
\item \code{tileApply(token, missing, ANY, ...)}
\item evaluation
}

\strong{\code{x} and \code{y} case:}
\enumerate{
\item \code{tileApply(ANY, ANY, ANY)}
\item \code{redispatch_tileapply(mydata1, mytiles, param_xy = "x")}
\item \code{redispatch_tileapply(ANY, ANY, param_xy = "x", ...)}
\item \code{tileApply(token, ANY, ANY)}
\item \code{redispatch_tileapply(mydata2, mytiles, param_xy = "y")}
\item \code{redispatch_tileapply(ANY, ANY, param_xy = "y", ...)}
\item \code{tileApply(token, token, plan)}
\item evaluation
}

\strong{This allows:}
\itemize{
\item Data type-specific preprocessing, checks, and additional params to be
injected into the \code{tileApply()} call without having to interact with the core
\code{tileApply()} logic for tilewise data selection and parallelization
\item Avoid duplicating data type-specific handling code for each of the following
\code{tileApply()} signature types:
\itemize{
\item \verb{(mydata, missing, tiles)}
\item \verb{(mydata, ANY, tiles)}
\item \verb{(token, mydata, tiles)}
}
}

Instead, extending developers only have to provide code for a single
\code{redispatch_tileapply(myclass, tiles)} signature, and
\code{tileApply()} <-> \code{redispatch_tileapply()} calls will iteratively apply the
appropriate preprocessing and x or y routed params.

The \code{x} and \code{y} routing and \code{token} coercion are also handled by the
\code{redispatch_tileapply(ANY, ANY)} method.
}

